#
# lucid-ddos post-lab quiz (Labtainer quiz format, True/False questions; see the Labtainer lab designer
# guide, section 7). Take it after the post-incident report:
#   on the Labtainer VM:        quiz -l lucid-ddos -q postlab    (self-check, answers are not saved)
#   in the server terminal:     python3 quiz.py postlab          (answers go to the instructor)
# server/quiz/postlab.quiz must stay identical to this file (tools/static_checks.py checks it).
#
0, Preface,
"Post-lab quiz: what the experiments and the live incident showed. Answer T (true) or F (false) \
from your own results."
1, TrueFalse, "Without normalization, features with large raw values such as the TCP acknowledgement number can dominate the training.", T,
"Raw header values live on very different scales (feature dominance), which makes training unstable.",
"Raw header values live on very different scales; the large ones dominate the gradients (feature dominance), as Experiment 1 shows."
2, TrueFalse, "In Experiment 2 the model with the IP addresses (lucid_ids) kept its accuracy when the attacker and client addresses were swapped.", F,
"Its accuracy fell to about 40 %: it had memorised the addresses.",
"On test_newaddr its accuracy fell to about 40 %: the model had learned the addresses (shortcut learning), not the traffic."
3, TrueFalse, "In Experiment 3, keeping only n = 3 packets per flow gave a lower accuracy than n = 100.", T,
"About 79 % instead of almost 100 %: three packets cut away the part of the flow that shows the attack.",
"With n = 3 the accuracy was about 79 % against almost 100 % for n = 100: too few packets cut away the part of the flow that shows the attack."
4, TrueFalse, "In Experiment 4 the SYN threshold flagged almost every flow of the distributed attack (50 bots).", F,
"It flagged nothing: every bot stayed under the per-source threshold.",
"Its accuracy of about 50 % means it flagged nothing: every bot stayed under the per-source threshold."
5, TrueFalse, "Permutation importance measures how much the accuracy drops when the values of one feature are shuffled.", T,
"A feature whose shuffling hurts the accuracy is one the model relies on.",
"Permutation importance shuffles one feature at a time and measures the drop in accuracy (Experiment 5)."
6, TrueFalse, "A model trained only on the CIC-DDoS2019 sample also detects the attacks of the lab network reliably.", F,
"On the lab network it caught almost none of the attacks: a model must be validated on the network it protects.",
"On the lab network its accuracy was about 43 % and it caught almost none of the attacks (Experiment 6): a model must be validated on the network it protects."
7, TrueFalse, "Two runs whose 95 % confidence intervals overlap cannot be called different with confidence.", T,
"Overlapping intervals mean the difference may be due to the particular test samples.",
"When the intervals overlap, the difference between the two runs may come from the particular test samples, so it cannot be claimed with confidence."
8, TrueFalse, "In the live test the legitimate client had to be blocked as well to stop the attack.", F,
"Only the two attacker addresses were blocked; the client kept its access.",
"Only the two attacker addresses were blocked with iptables DROP rules; the legitimate client kept its access, which is what a good detector should achieve."
